$DIR/main.p:20:19: out[i] is written by the tasks started at main.p:20 at the same time as each other
    Each call of s.Task at main.p:20 starts a task, and they run at the same
    time. Each writes out[i] (main.p:20), and another reads it (main.p:20). Two
    tasks using one place at once, one of them writing, is a data race: an
    update can be lost, and a value larger than a word can be read half written.
    in[i] and out[i] are reached through different views, which may point to the
    same memory: the compiler cannot tell where the memory they look at comes
    from, and both may hold values of type int. Two slices of one type are
    accepted when the task uses each only as x[i], with the same loop variable
    i: the program then checks, before each task starts, that the slices start
    at the same element or do not overlap.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Lock a mutex around every use of out[i]:
        mu.Lock()
        ...
        mu.Unlock()
    Or, for a number, use an atomic type such as atomic.Int64.
    Or give each task its own element: results[i] = ..., where i is the loop
    variable.
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3402" for a longer explanation)
